   "Second my hopes, Your Paternity, and do not leave me idle in Italy, where to my great shame so many departures of our Fathers are seen every day; nor show yourself partial (pardon the affection if I use such a word), remembering others and not me. I would write in blood, that it might bear witness to what I desire and be fulfilled the sooner; but I trust in the charity with which Your Paternity is wont ever to console his sons, that you will not forget me. I stand awaiting the notice as the final completion and most desired end to which all my labors are directed. And finally, in your holy sacrifices before His Divine Majesty, do not forget me, who have so great need."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy son and servant in the Lord — Leonardo Cinami. From Naples, September 9, 1628."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, September 9, 1628",
  "date_only": "September 9, 1628",
  "year": 1628,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 106,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2842"
 },
 "70": {
  "number": 70,
  "byline_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672) — October 1628",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 738, 305",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Having already three times proposed to Your Paternity the desire which His Divine Majesty has deigned to give me for the Indies — and having, by my negligence and carelessness, not sought a response — now, so as not to have to render account to God of having trampled His Inspirations by not cooperating with them, I have resolved, as I do, to pray Your Paternity to deign, if you judge the fulfillment of this desire to be in any way, for the Reasons stated at other times, a service of His Divine Majesty, to deign to grant it to me.",
   "And if even now, by reason of age and studies etc., it should not seem fitting to you, at least at the end of the present Year of Physics — which I believe would be a most convenient time — I pray you to grant it to me, for that love which you bear to your sons and the desire you have for their perfection, and for all their greater good and glory of God.",
   "Trusting in the divine Omnipotence and goodness, and in the intercession of the Most Blessed Virgin, who will give me the spiritual and bodily virtue and strength necessary for this — with which, praying His Divine Majesty for abundant graces to bear the burden he bears, I offer myself to you most indifferent to all things, to be placed and employed in whatever shall be the greater service of His Divine Majesty and the salvation of mine and of souls."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Most Reverend Paternity's most humble son and servant in Christ — Francesco Bressani."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672)",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "October 1628",
  "date_only": "October 1628",
  "year": 1628,
  "month": 10,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 107,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2731"
 },
 "71": {
  "number": 71,
  "byline_raw": "Gabriele Archinto — Milan, December 25, 1629",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 738, 402",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "So great is the longing and desire that Our Lord has communicated to me to go and assist the souls who remain in the Indies, and to shed my blood for love of my Lord, who first shed His for me, that after having waited two years, He has now so increased it in me that, did I not offer myself at the feet of Your Paternity, most ready for its execution, I would judge myself to be manifestly contradicting the divine will, which calls me to great things, as appears from the signal favors by which He has delivered me from many and various infirmities and manifest dangers of life, endured even from the cradle, and [by endowing me with] a constitution and nature well suited to the labors and hardships of those countries — not without manifest indication that He has reserved me to spend my life and shed my blood to His glory and in aid of souls. To these corporal favors He has added many spiritual ones through His infinite goodness, among which I count as great His having called and wanted me in the Company at the age of fourteen, and, by His most liberal grace and to my great confusion in corresponding to it, having preserved me from every mortal sin, and given me grace and a living desire not only to offer myself to Him in Religion with the holy vows, but also to the labors and sufferings even to the last drop of blood, amid the swords and flames of Japan. And therefore I supplicate Your Paternity with all sincerity and affection to ratify the will of God, and to cooperate, so that I may render to my Lord that life which He has preserved to me from so many dangers, that I may sacrifice it to Him in those places and at those times in which persecution rages most cruelly against Christians and preachers of the Gospel, for such is and always shall be my one desire; and while I attend to providing myself with solid virtues and learning for the service of God in those countries, I shall be awaiting, together with His holy blessing, the long-desired grace, as I once more supplicate Your Paternity for the only consolation of your unworthy son. I wish you, at the close, the happiest feasts of Holy Christmas, and I commend myself greatly to your prayers and holy sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, unworthy son and servant in Christ — Gabriele Archinto of the Company of JESUS. From Milan, December 25, 1629."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gabriele Archinto",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, December 25, 1629",
  "date_only": "December 25, 1629",
  "year": 1629,
  "month": 12,
  "day": 25,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 108,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2681"
 },
 "72": {
  "number": 72,
  "byline_raw": "Antonio Heredia — Massa, March 2, 1630",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 27",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "His Divine Majesty having, from the very beginning of my entrance, enkindled in my heart a lively and ardent desire to shed my blood in the Indies for His love, and having preserved it in me throughout all the time of my Novitiate; now that I have been destined by holy Obedience to the study of Rhetoric, and with the occasion of the Father Procurator of Ethiopia who was here, and with the fervor of these brothers, I have judged (lest I seem ungrateful to His Divine Majesty for so great a benefit) to make Your Paternity, as my true Father, aware of it, praying you with the greatest affection I can, and for the love of the Most Holy Virgin, that you will number me among the others destined for that mission, although I am most unworthy of it, so that, should the occasion offer itself, you will not fail to console me. With this I close, commending myself to the holy sacrifices of Your Paternity and seeking your blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Antonio Heredia. From Massa, March 27, 1630."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Antonio Heredia",
  "place_raw": "Massa",
  "date_raw": "Massa, March 2, 1630",
  "date_only": "March 2, 1630",
  "year": 1630,
  "month": 3,
  "day": 2,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 110,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2749"
 },
 "73": {
  "number": 73,
  "byline_raw": "Gabriele Archinto — Milan, January 12, 1631",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 739, 101",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "A year ago I gave Your Paternity account of the desire which God our Lord communicated to me to spend my life and shed my blood for love of my Lord, and for the salvation of souls, in Japan; therefore, lest it perhaps be thought that in the midst of so many miseries it had been extinguished or cooled, I have wished to renew the request made to you, especially since the desire has been renewed in me, and indeed increased all the more. The tokens of the divine will signified to Your Paternity in my other letter I believe to be so many and so clear that there is no longer room to doubt it; but if any doubt remained, let the miracle worked by the Lord in preserving me from death in the midst of the poisonous contagion wholly remove it; for having dealt so intimately with a fellow student of mine stricken with the plague two days before his death, assisting him in everything, even to holding his forehead for a great while, while he burned all over with the pestilential fever, I cannot believe that God has reserved my life to me for any other purpose than that I might render it to Him more lavishly and gloriously amid the flames and butchery of Japan. I shall not extend myself further in signifying to you the ardent longing which Our Lord communicates to me thereof, sufficing to say that I burn and am consumed; and holding it for certain that the same God who gives it to me will also manifest it to the heart of Your Paternity, I close, humbly asking your holy blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, son and servant in Christ — Gabriel Archinto. From Milan, January 12, 1631."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gabriele Archinto",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, January 12, 1631",
  "date_only": "January 12, 1631",
  "year": 1631,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 12,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 111,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2682"
 },
 "74": {
  "number": 74,
  "byline_raw": "Gabriele Archinto — Milan, March 2, 1631",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 739, 108",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I shall seem troublesome to Your Paternity for so often recalling to you the ardent longing communicated to me by the Lord to shed my blood in Japan for His love and for the salvation of souls. It is true, Father; I confess it; it is little since I wrote to you, but I can no longer bear it; from the last letter I wrote you until now I have restrained myself, not to weary you with my letters, but now the desire presses too hard, and I can wait no more; therefore I have wished with this one to lay open my heart to you, so that, should occasion offer itself to dispatch people to those parts, I may not be among the last to be sent. Everything seems nothing to me, and there is nothing that fully satisfies me, until the happy lot of going to Japan falls to me. Every hour, in every place, and in whatever action, I remember most loving Japan; even in sleep itself it often seems to me that I am placed on the cross, awaiting the final lance wound from the executioner, and so great is the sadness I feel afterward, seeing it all to be vain, as was great before the joy which so long-desired an end gave me: in short, I think of nothing but Japan, and all that is not Japan seems nothing to me. So then, the indications of the divine will being so manifest, as Your Paternity may infer from my other letters, with the addition of the ardent longing which is increased in me daily by the same Lord, I pray Your Paternity not to despise the prayers of your unworthy son, but rather to ratify the divine will; and I meanwhile, daily supplicating His Divine Majesty for the fulfillment of this my desire, shall be awaiting, together with your holy blessing, the long-desired grace; and at the close I commend myself with all my heart to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, most unworthy son and servant in Christ — Gabriele Archinto of the Company of Jesus. From Milan, March 2, 1631."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gabriele Archinto",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, March 2, 1631",
  "date_only": "March 2, 1631",
  "year": 1631,
  "month": 3,
  "day": 2,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 112,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2683"
 },
 "75": {
  "number": 75,
  "byline_raw": "Orazio Vimercati — Milan, November 5, 1631",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 739, 159",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Some time ago I wrote to Your Paternity of the vocation to the Indies which Our Lord deigned, from the very beginning of my entrance into Religion, to give me, and of its effect I obtained good hope from Your Paternity. Now I write anew, both to remind Your Paternity of it and to supplicate you anew, seeing that God calls me thither, by preserving the vocation in me and by having preserved me amid so many dangers of the plague, in which I was at Nice; among which dangers, once finding myself in the public lazaretto with suspicion of the plague, I promised God that, if I came out safe, I would dedicate myself wholly to His service in the Indies, and to the Most Holy Virgin that I would go to visit her at Loreto; for this reason, the time not being opportune, I say nothing further of the latter, but of the former, desiring to receive some assurance, I say that, although when I wrote I offered myself with all my heart to Your Paternity, now more than ever I offer myself for every part of the Indies, and chiefly for those where most is suffered; and I supplicate Your Paternity to be pleased to fulfill this my desire, born of the will I have to do penance for my sins, by endeavoring to make up for the negligence I have had in my own salvation by diligence in that of others. Which I hope to obtain from Your Paternity, for the love of God, I prepare myself for every occasion of departure, assisted by the holy sacrifices of Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant in Christ and unworthy son — Orazio Vimercati. Milan, November 5, 1631."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Orazio Vimercati",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, November 5, 1631",
  "date_only": "November 5, 1631",
  "year": 1631,
  "month": 11,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 114,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2706"
 },
 "76": {
  "number": 76,
  "byline_raw": "Daniello Bartoli — Parma, February 2, 1632",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 739, 179",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The most ardent desire for the Indies, which by the infinite goodness of the Lord God continues ever greater in me, compels me to ask you for the grace thereof again, as I have done at other times, so now more than ever insistently. I am exceedingly ashamed before the Lord God to utter a word to ask of Your Paternity so great a favor, of which I know myself to be in every respect most unworthy; nevertheless, I do not think that the Lord God has communicated to me in vain such a burning desire to spend all that I have of strength, talent, spirit, and life to labor and to die for the defense of our holy faith and for the help of the unbelievers. Let Your Paternity but send me to whatever part of the world pleases you most, to whatever nations, and to do whatever you will; I am most ready for everything; would that I were sufficient. I know that these times are less opportune for the execution of what I ask: let this then serve to obtain from Your Paternity the grace of departure at the first occasion that offers itself to you. And since you cannot have sufficient motive to send me from my goodness and sufficiency, for I am most base and most unfit for so great an undertaking, I supplicate you, by the goodness of the Lord God, to be pleased, so to speak, to venture me, or to place me as an addition to those others who will be most worthy to go. My most beloved Father, I commend myself to you for the grace with all possible affection and humility; and I pray His Divine Majesty to give you, with the fulfillment of all His goods, also this goodwill toward me, while I most humbly kiss your feet, prostrate on the ground."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, most unworthy son in the Lord — Daniele Bartoli. Parma, February 2, 1632."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Daniello Bartoli",
  "place_raw": "Parma",
  "date_raw": "Parma, February 2, 1632",
  "date_only": "February 2, 1632",
  "year": 1632,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 2,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 115,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2861"
 },
 "77": {
  "number": 77,
  "byline_raw": "Gabriele Archinto — Milan, January 29, 1633",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 739, 225",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Mindful of your promise to me, as benign as it was liberal, made in several letters, concerning the fulfillment of my vocation already several times manifested to you, I have not wished to fail to renew the memory of it in you, especially since it seems to me that now the occasion offers itself to complete the work, through the offering made, as I have understood, by Your Paternity to the Supreme Pontiff of a hundred laborers to cultivate those vineyards; therefore I pray you, prostrate at your feet, to be pleased to console me this time, especially since it seems to me that this is also a fitting time for me, finding myself in the last year of philosophy, so that it would be no disturbance at all to my studies, and I could be more apt to learn, together with the language of the country, whatever else might be imposed upon me. I pray Your Paternity therefore to be pleased to hear my words and my enkindled desire, without regard to the imperfections of this your unworthy son, assuring you that now I shall begin a new life, with a new religious spirit, so that the Lord God may deign to grant me the desired grace; for which I once more supplicate Your Paternity, as my only consolation; and at the close I pray you for your holy blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, unworthy son and servant — Gabriele Archinto. From Milan, January 29, 1633."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gabriele Archinto",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, January 29, 1633",
  "date_only": "January 29, 1633",
  "year": 1633,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 29,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 117,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2684"
 },
 "78": {
  "number": 78,
  "byline_raw": "Gabriele Archinto — Milan, May 12, 1633",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 739, 238",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "It seems to me now that the time has come to effectuate my so many times manifested desire to be once sent to the country so longed for by me, of the Indies. I shall not repeat a long enumeration concerning the beginning, progress, and end, the motives and signs of the Divine will concerning this mission, supposing it all manifest to Your Paternity by means of my other letters; only I pray you, since the signs of the Divine vocation seem so manifest, not to fail to fulfill what the Lord God designs to do through me, with the addition also to this of the goodwill shown me by the Father Procurator of those parts. Of your grace, do not deprive this unworthy son of yours of so great a consolation; which while I am awaiting, I shall endeavor to make myself a more worthy subject of that mission, asking you therefore for your holy blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, unworthy son and servant — Gabriele Archinto. From Milan, May 12, 1633."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gabriele Archinto",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, May 12, 1633",
  "date_only": "May 12, 1633",
  "year": 1633,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 12,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 118,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2685"
 },
 "79": {
  "number": 79,
  "byline_raw": "Daniello Bartoli — Parma, May 16, 1633",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 739, 239",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Many times I have offered myself to Your Paternity to spend my labors, my blood, and my life in the conversion of souls among the unbelievers and heretics; and I have always had such answer as showed that Your Paternity's good will to grant me the grace lacked nothing but the occasion. Now that the Father Procurator of Peru comes thither to supplicate Your Paternity also, among other things, that you grant him some of Ours for those countries; here I am, Father, most ready at every sign; most desirous, if such be the will of the Lord God, to consume myself wholly in the sufferings and dangers incurred in the conversion of the unbelievers. I do not pray you to send me to Peru, or to any other country by name, for I will never add a word that would take from me a total indifference and from Your Paternity a supreme liberty to dispose of me for whatever country pleases you. Only, I signify to you that the desire which has always grown in me since ten years ago it came to me is not to change country, but to spend all my labor, and a thousand lives if I had so many, for the propagation of the holy faith; and where dangers are greater, and greater occasion there is to suffer and to die amid the hardships, or to be killed for this end, thither I feel myself, by Divine grace, the more animated to go; be it Japan, England, China, the Mogul, or any other country. But let it be at least some one; for thus, prostrate at your feet with all the greater affection and humility, I supplicate you for it: praying you, moreover, to give me your paternal blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, most unworthy son and servant in the Lord — Daniele Bartoli. From Parma, May 16, 1633."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Daniello Bartoli",
  "place_raw": "Parma",
  "date_raw": "Parma, May 16, 1633",
  "date_only": "May 16, 1633",
  "year": 1633,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 16,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 119,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2862"
 },
 "80": {
  "number": 80,
  "byline_raw": "Orazio Vimercati — Milan, May 17, 1633",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 739, 242",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Your Paternity having, to my petition to go to the Indies, already twice answered that there was no occasion, since at that time there was no thought of sending laborers to those parts; I supplicate Your Paternity now that the occasion offers itself, to deign to satisfy this my desire, with which I entered the Company, in the hope that it should once be fulfilled. True it is that I recognize in myself, not even from afar, that heap of virtues which Your Paternity says in one of yours is necessary to such laborers; nevertheless I hope in the grace of the Lord that, as by His goodness He has hitherto maintained in me the vocation to the Indies, so He may also give me sufficient strength to correspond to it, whenever it pleases Him to inspire Your Paternity to give me, with the leave, your blessing, which prostrate at your feet, as an unworthy son, I ask in your holy sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, servant in Christ, and unworthy son — Orazio Vimercati. From Milan, May 17, 1633."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Orazio Vimercati",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, May 17, 1633",
  "date_only": "May 17, 1633",
  "year": 1633,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 17,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 120,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2707"
 },
 "81": {
  "number": 81,
  "byline_raw": "Archinto, Gabriele — Milan, June 28, 1633",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 739, 251",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father, Our Father in Christ, most worthy of observance",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The continual goad of my divine vocation, which ever urges me on, together with the kindness of Your Paternity shown me in other letters of yours, has given me the assurance to trouble you once again with my tedious letters, all of which end in asking you for the longed-for fulfillment of the so-desired mission of the Indies; and now I ask it all the more warmly, inasmuch as I feel myself burning more than usual, particularly in these holy days of the renewal of vows, with the desire for the end I have so long sighed for. And the last contests of the resolution to send me with the present Father Procurator seem nearer, whose departure, as I understood from him, would not be long delayed. I beg you therefore not to hinder so great a consolation to my soul, which with a single nod you can so abundantly grant; and while, in the renewal tomorrow of the oblation of the vows already made, I shall pray the Lord for this, I beg you for the customary earnest of every grace and favor, which is your holy blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, son and servant in Christ — Gabriele Archinto. From Milan, June 28, 1633."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Archinto, Gabriele",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, June 28, 1633",
  "date_only": "June 28, 1633",
  "year": 1633,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 28,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 121,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2686"
 },
 "82": {
  "number": 82,
  "byline_raw": "Archinto, Alessandro — Milan, April 11, 1634",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 740, 78",
  "salutation": [
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Since one of the causes that moved me rather to choose the religious state in the Company than in another order was the hope of obtaining one day from my superiors the mission of the Indies, and in particular of those places where there was the greatest occasion of suffering, for this reason, having communicated this vocation of mine many times with my spiritual fathers, and having often commended myself from my heart to the Divine Majesty that it might make known to me its holy will, I have at last resolved to seek with all my power to obtain so great a grace, and I come with this letter of mine to pray Your Paternity to fulfill my desires. My inclination was from the first for Japan, but since I see the great difficulty there is in obtaining so great a mission, I offer myself to Your Paternity for that of Paraguay, and I await, with the coming of the Father Procurator, that Your Paternity will heed my petitions. For which, begging you with all possible affection, and remembering myself as your most humble son, I commend my miseries to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, unworthy son and servant in the Lord — Alessandro Archinto of the Company of Jesus. From Milan, April 11, 1634."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Archinto, Alessandro",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, April 11, 1634",
  "date_only": "April 11, 1634",
  "year": 1634,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 11,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 122,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2679"
 },
 "83": {
  "number": 83,
  "byline_raw": "Taverna, Francesco — 1635",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 740, 326",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I have always had a great desire to help, as much as I can with the grace of His Divine Majesty, the souls of men, with the desire to go to the Indies; and therefore, by the bowels of Jesus Christ and of His most holy Mother, I supplicate with all my heart Your Paternity to place me in the roll of those who go to the Indies, so that I may one day lay down this miserable life for that Lord who laid down His so noble one for me, a miserable and unworthy creature."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most humble servant and son — Francesco Taverna."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Taverna, Francesco",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "1635",
  "date_only": "1635",
  "year": 1635,
  "month": null,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 123,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2704"
 },
 "84": {
  "number": 84,
  "byline_raw": "Archinto, Gabriele — Milan, January 2, 1635",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 740, 274",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "On the occasion of the good beginning of the year, which I pray may be most happy for Your Paternity, I did not wish to neglect to represent to you anew the vehemence of my desire, which grows day by day and will torment me until I am given some nearer hope than I have yet had. I spoke this morning with Father Magaglianes, who with his great charity much enlarged my heart, assuring me that I would obtain from Your Paternity whatever I knew how to ask in this matter. I have recourse therefore to Your Paternity with all confidence, begging you that — since this vocation has now for many years been detained and nourished by me as no small sign of the divine will, and since such good heart has been given me by Father Magaglianes — I beg you, I say, at last to console me by reserving for me a little corner among those who, as I understand, are to depart at the end of this year. For which, begging you anew, I anew wish you a happy beginning of this year, and humbly ask your holy blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, most humble son and servant in the Lord — Gabriele Archinto. From Milan, January 2, 1635."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Archinto, Gabriele",
  "place_raw": "Milan",
  "date_raw": "Milan, January 2, 1635",
  "date_only": "January 2, 1635",
  "year": 1635,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 2,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 124,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2687"
 },
 "85": {
  "number": 85,
  "byline_raw": "Bartoli, Daniello (1608-1685) — Parma, October 5, 1635",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 740, 363",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I have consoled all this year my desire with the hope of the arrival there of the Father Procurator of Cochin, to whom Your Paternity promised Father Antonio Magaglianes to give me as companion for the mission of the Indies. Now that he has arrived, here I am, after so many other times, prostrate anew at your feet, where with all the affection of my soul, for the love of the holy Father Xavier, I supplicate you to console at last my desire to consume myself wholly in the aid of those barbarians, and to shed even, should the Lord God do me the grace, my blood for His love and in witness of His holy faith. This desire has remained ever most lively in my heart, and has grown since I entered this holy order, twelve years now. This I offer to Your Paternity, and for its fulfillment I supplicate you with all my heart. Do not delay, for the love of God, to a later occasion to do me the grace, but from this hour give what remains of my life wholly to the holy Apostle Xavier and consecrate it to the good of the Indies; where, if you grant me to go there, I shall never cease to show myself grateful to Your Paternity, offering in recognition of so great a grace the best part of the labors that apostolic life brings with it. Wherewith, in conclusion, prostrate anew at your feet, I ask most humbly, with the desired grace, your paternal blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, most unworthy servant and son — Daniele Bartoli. From Parma, October 5, 1635."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Bartoli, Daniello (1608-1685)",
  "place_raw": "Parma",
  "date_raw": "Parma, October 5, 1635",
  "date_only": "October 5, 1635",
  "year": 1635,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 125,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2863"
 },
 "86": {
  "number": 86,
  "byline_raw": "Serafini, Fortunato — April 20, 1636",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 740, 432",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "After having had recourse to Your Paternity many times in person, and also with written letters, being unable now without great difficulty to come in person to set forth, or to confirm, my ancient desire, given me by the Lord God together with that of the religious life, to leave this world wholly, that I may carry out the Lord's will — one might say in another world — if with nothing else, at least with a prompt obedience and an entire oblation of my own life, however it is to be employed, I come with this letter to supplicate anew with all affection that Your Paternity would cooperate with the divine will, which I have experienced with so many and such certain demonstrations that nothing is lacking but the approbation of Your Paternity — an infallible proof, indeed a most evident sign, of it. I wrote at length two years ago, in a Latin letter of mine, the motives and progress of this vocation of mine, so it is not necessary to repeat them; and even so, that I may not further occupy Your Paternity, occupied as you are with so many other things, I add only that daily the affection toward that place warms and inflames ever more, and the Indies stand almost continually impressed upon my heart, in which I dwell often in thought; and I find peace in no other end than in a death so many times imagined, and as many times almost promised and accepted. I have made the Company's ordinary experiments of the pilgrimage, the hospital, Christian doctrine; I have tried also some little mission in the squares of Rome and at Frascati, as a prelude to the great ones; and for all this I am neither dead nor even fallen ill, and I find myself now in the seminary in the best of health, by the grace of God, so that I see there can be no impediment. I was moved to make this new instance by seeing my fellow disciples of fewer years in religion, but of truly greater spirit, sent, while I remain here. May the Holy Spirit move the heart of Your Paternity, as I pray and hope, and I make you profound reverence."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, unworthy servant in the Lord — Fortunato Serafini of the Company of Jesus. April 20, 1636."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Serafini, Fortunato",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "April 20, 1636",
  "date_only": "April 20, 1636",
  "year": 1636,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 20,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 126,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2857"
 },
 "87": {
  "number": 87,
  "byline_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672) — May 1636",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 740, 327",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "As I never feel my lukewarmness more kindled than when that holy desire is revived in me which, against all my merit, the Holy Spirit kindled in my heart — as I have always believed — for the holy mission of the Indies, and as I never conceive any good desire without this one at once renewing itself with it; so I think myself obliged often to turn my thoughts to it and to propose it warmly, until Your Paternity, to whom after God I must turn, grants me its effect, lest my many demerits hinder the execution of the divine will and the possibility of in fact more abundantly sharing the divine liberality which, with its holy inspirations, has already offered me favors so great. All the more would I wish to be able to express to Your Paternity the liveliness of this desire, the more I am convinced by experience that whatever little good I think of doing, I at once prove a particular recompense of graces from that Lord, so liberal that He promised not to leave even a cup of water without reward. This is all my confidence in this matter: that if, with His blessing and that of Your Paternity, I should undertake this holy voyage, I would receive some particular measure of graces, with whose aid I might spend all the little that the Lord will give me of life with that spirit which I have not had until now and which is necessary to a true son of the Company. Set me, Your Paternity, in this occasion of so great a good, for that affection and desire which you have for the salvation of your sons, even the most unworthy and miserable.",
   "And while I add nothing more, that I may not weary you further, believe that if I know how to express little desire in this letter, the desire I keep in my heart is not little, for the reasons at other times proposed to you; which I pray that the working of the Holy Spirit, who in these days descends from heaven, may make efficacious. And while I pray that you may have great abundance of Him, I hope to have some share in it myself through the prayers of Your Paternity. Quam Deus [...] (which God [...])"
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Unworthy son and servant in Christ — Francesco Bressani. May 1636."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672)",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "May 1636",
  "date_only": "May 1636",
  "year": 1636,
  "month": 5,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 128,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2732"
 },
 "88": {
  "number": 88,
  "byline_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672) — July 1636",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 740, 444",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "There is not yet extinguished amid the ice of my so many failings that little warmth with which the Lord kindled my heart when He gave me the holy desire of the Indies. It is perhaps that little [of ... covered ...] but there will come a time [...] when [it shall appear luminous]. Meanwhile may Your Paternity [...] me, if not with a final concession of the grace, at least with a firm hope of it; and I shall be eternally mindful of such a benefit, which I wholly believe pertains to the salvation of my soul, and I shall be under a more particular obligation to pray the Lord for Your Paternity. Quam Deus [...] (which God [...])"
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Most Reverend Paternity, most humble servant and unworthy son in Christ — Francesco Bressani. July 1636."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672)",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "July 1636",
  "date_only": "July 1636",
  "year": 1636,
  "month": 7,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 130,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2733"
 },
 "89": {
  "number": 89,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Caltanissetta, June 8, 1637",
  "language": "Latin",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 27",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "It is a wonder with what joys my soul has been inflamed since you recently, by your letters, stirred in me the expectation of an Indian expedition; but while this care sweetly wastes and turns me, as to what year you will at last relegate me, I would very much like to know. For why should I deny it? A flame divinely kindled in me since I was a little boy so rouses me day and night that I plainly waste away with desire; whence, if I learn that I have been drawn far off from you for a long time, I shall judge my life altogether bitter; and indeed I cannot but be tormented when an ardor so lofty in its conception, strengthened by the age of so many years, falls nearly to nothing (for I understand it is put off from day to day). But if my letter has perhaps slipped out over-boldly, may you, my good and easy Father, grant pardon to my ambition, and lend me, I pray, that hand which alone can heap upon me the fullness of joys, whose image, wandering even in my dreams, so tickles me with delight that every moment seems slow to me. But since I know your will to be well disposed toward the zeal of your sons, there is no reason for me to add a word; nay, if in your most limpid conferences with God you regard me, I trust that I shall obtain what I desire. Farewell."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Given on the eighth of June, in the year 1637 from the Virgin's childbirth — Marius Finechiaro. From Caltanissetta, June 8, 1637."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Caltanissetta",
  "date_raw": "Caltanissetta, June 8, 1637",
  "date_only": "June 8, 1637",
  "year": 1637,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 8,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 131,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2745"
 },
 "90": {
  "number": 90,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — St. [illegible], October 9, 1637",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 47",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "It is many years since I wrote to Your Paternity, asking you as a grace to destine me to the mission of the East Indies; but partly from finding myself in the midst of my course of studies, and partly from my sins, was I the cause that I did not merit so signal a favor. Now finding myself at the end of my theology, no longer encumbered with studies, I return to remind Your Paternity of it, the more so since it is said that the procurator of those parts is shortly to come here. I therefore cast myself at your feet, and most humbly pray you, with all the affection I can and with all the force I know how, that you will make me set sail for the Indies as soon as may be; and I ask you this grace for all you love the salvation of my soul, for all you desire that I make satisfaction in some part for the infinite multitude of my sins, in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ). I shall never cease to importune Your Paternity with letters, and His Divine Majesty with prayers, until I obtain the going thither; but the desire you have for my salvation, I believe, will move you, though I do not merit it, to do me this grace — the greatest I can desire in this life. Wherewith, praying that you may have infinite years of life from heaven for the good of the Company and of the whole world, I end."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant and most unworthy son in the Lord — Leonardo Cinami. October 9, 1637."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "St. [illegible]",
  "date_raw": "St. [illegible], October 9, 1637",
  "date_only": "October 9, 1637",
  "year": 1637,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 132,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2843"
 },
 "91": {
  "number": 91,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Caltanissetta, November 12, 1637",
  "language": "Latin",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 67",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Since the flames of my ardor for the Indian enterprise are doubled in me more and more each day, I feel myself sweetly and unceasingly pricked to write to You again with greater care; but what argument will a frail page with faltering little letters set before You from a mind covered, indeed almost wholly consumed, by the little flames of heaven? Yet I will not suffer my spirit to despair; for that most sweet patronage of the Mother of God, through which I understand all my concerns to be sustained, will at once receive my cause with kindly brow, she who strikes into me even now, by her present deity, a desire so great that I plainly leap for joy and am carried aloft by divine longings. For although You keep me as Your intermediary in every place, yet set beside the Indian regions all things grow cheap to me; for my spirit, even from far away, disports itself among them as in its own lakes: and if, with no interval of time intervening, You will open to me the ready entrance that is shut, and suffer me to commit myself to the way, I shall straightway be overwhelmed with the most vehement joys.",
   "Wherefore, before the cares of good letters grow cold in me, I contend the more keenly with You, Father, to answer my wishes — I whose ears and spirit are raised in expectation of it. I will not have You send back any letter to me unless it be brimming with rich hope, a first swallow announcing that You are most ready to fulfill my vows: and if You will comply the sooner, a heap of joy will befall me; but if otherwise than I desire should befall, I shall be tormented with unceasing longings. Finally, since I rightly know that You wish me well for my own sake, I shall proceed no further: only that You promote my vows, whole and entire, whence I confidently trust to gather a full harvest of my desire."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Marius Finechiarus. Caltanissetta, November 12, 1637."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Caltanissetta",
  "date_raw": "Caltanissetta, November 12, 1637",
  "date_only": "November 12, 1637",
  "year": 1637,
  "month": 11,
  "day": 12,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 133,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2747"
 },
 "92": {
  "number": 92,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, January 16, 1638",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 79",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The great desire, which Our Lord in His infinite mercy deigns to communicate to me, to go to the Indies, compels me to trouble Your Paternity with this letter. I pray you, therefore, with the greatest warmth I know and can, to grant me this favor, which, after my vocation, I esteem the greatest that can be granted me in this life; and I take the boldness to make you a fresh plea because, as I wrote you last time, this being the last year of my studies, I find myself already clear for the voyage. I know how little worthy I am of this favor, but for all that I hope in the blood of Our Lord Jesus Christ, and in the kindness of Your Paternity, that you will deign to oblige me, to give me occasion to make satisfaction for my past failings, which, the greater they are, the greater my obligation to do something in the service of God. Meanwhile I am praying that Heaven grant you infinite years of life for the benefit of the Company, and I pray you to grant me the favor of your blessing, which I would have come together with the patent for the voyage."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most humble servant and son in Christ — Leonardo Cinami. Naples, January 16, 1638."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, January 16, 1638",
  "date_only": "January 16, 1638",
  "year": 1638,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 16,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 135,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2844"
 },
 "93": {
  "number": 93,
  "byline_raw": "Dempsterus, Francesco — Spoleto, May 15, 1638",
  "language": "Latin",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 98",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I, the least of all men, ardently desire to be sent to India."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Franciscus Dempsterus. Spoleto, May 15, 1638."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Dempsterus, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Spoleto",
  "date_raw": "Spoleto, May 15, 1638",
  "date_only": "May 15, 1638",
  "year": 1638,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 15,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 136,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2839"
 },
 "94": {
  "number": 94,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Palermo, June 9, 1638",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 52",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "As great as is my consolation at hearing of the happy lot of those Fathers, presently called by Your Paternity to so blessed an enterprise, so much the more is the grief quickened in my soul at finding myself left out of such an election, so long sighed for by me; wherefore I can do nothing else but weep for my faults, which doubtless accuse me before the Divine countenance as most unworthy of so abundant a grace as the Mission of the Indies and suffering for Christ. Yet I trust that the Most Blessed Virgin, as she once was pleased to kindle in me with so fervent an impulse this flame, will finally deign in her mercy to bring it to the desired end; praying also warmly Your Paternity to show me at least some small ray of hope with a brief line of extraordinary consolation — grieving as I do beyond measure that I cannot communicate to you fully with the pen the burning desire that my anxious soul feels in every part. With which I commend myself to you in the Holy Sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy son and servant in the Lord — Mario Finechiaro. Palermo, June 9, 1638."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, June 9, 1638",
  "date_only": "June 9, 1638",
  "year": 1638,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 137,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2750"
 },
 "95": {
  "number": 95,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Mineo, June 9, 1638",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 128",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The heavenly flame cannot be hidden, nor can one act so that its burning is not felt; hence I cannot but trouble Your Paternity with repeated letters, You who with a nod can calm it. My Father, the desire that the Lord kindled in me for the Mission of the Indies has grown so great that I hope the same Lord, with His Most Holy Mother, will move Your Paternity to console me; for the works of God are most perfect, and I feel myself urged to entreat you of it as much as I fear that, once the course of philosophy has begun next year, my desire, already ripe and brooking no more delay, may be prolonged for me. I pray you, therefore, that with the cool of the season the flame of my long affection may likewise be lightened; and at least, should Your Paternity command otherwise, do not delay this answer, so that with the hope of receiving it happily I may go on feeding and beguiling my desire. With which I offer myself to you, commending myself to the Holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant in the Lord and most unworthy son — Mario Finechiaro. Mineo, June 9, 1638."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Mineo",
  "date_raw": "Mineo, June 9, 1638",
  "date_only": "June 9, 1638",
  "year": 1638,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 138,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2753"
 },
 "96": {
  "number": 96,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, June 19, 1638",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 138",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I believe this letter of mine will have the fortune of finding Your Paternity in Rome, and I rejoice that it comes into your hands together with the others, so that they may strike a greater blow and obtain from Your Paternity what I hope has already been obtained from Heaven. I dare to say this because night and day I do nothing but penances and prayers to this end, and not only I, but countless others make prayers and penances for me, to move first God and then the heart of Your Paternity to place me among the number of the elect and assign me to the Indies. Oh, that I could reveal in some way the fire I feel in my heart, and the desire that God communicates to me! Reckon, Your Paternity, that there is no moment when I do not run to the Indies in thought; no word ever leaves my mouth that does not treat of those lands; I never close my eyes without dreaming of Japan, or the Ocean, or martyrdoms. In short, God has lifted me out of myself and out of Europe, and makes me dwell only in Asia among barbarians and infidels. Let Your Paternity make me follow God, who will have me at all costs, and be moved by those tears which I cannot show you, but which I shed continually for this grace before Saint Francis Xavier. I await the blessing of Your Paternity, which, though by no merit of mine deserved, is owed to my continual weeping, to prayer, to my most ardent desire, to the vow made to God with so much feeling, with the counsel of many holy men, with the leave of my Superiors, and lastly to the kindness of Your Paternity, from which I dare promise myself everything — all the more since going to the Indies is Your particular sentiment, as is read in Your letters. What use is it to spur us on, if then You would put the bridle on us just as we are about to run? With which I commend myself to the Holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Leonardo Cinami. Naples, June 19, 1638."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, June 19, 1638",
  "date_only": "June 19, 1638",
  "year": 1638,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 19,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 139,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2845"
 },
 "97": {
  "number": 97,
  "byline_raw": "Dempsterus, Francesco — Spoleto, July 31, 1638",
  "language": "Latin",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 188",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I, the vilest of mortals, have nothing in hope, desire, expectation, or love, except to be sent to India."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Franciscus Dempsterus. Spoleto, July 31, 1638."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Dempsterus, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Spoleto",
  "date_raw": "Spoleto, July 31, 1638",
  "date_only": "July 31, 1638",
  "year": 1638,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 31,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 141,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2840"
 },
 "98": {
  "number": 98,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Massa, November 25, 1638",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 261",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I ceased for some time to press Your Paternity for the mission of the Indies, from which for my sins I have deserved for this time to be excluded. But I do not believe that for this Your Paternity denies me leave to continue weeping before you, and to signify to you the desires that God gives me. I have already taken up Rhetoric, as Your Paternity and the Father Provincial have commanded me, and I would have done so with every other exercise, however lowly, had it been the will of obedience; nevertheless I pray you to be pleased that in this time too you give ear to His Divine Majesty, who calls me and prepares me for the other Mission, which they say should be near at hand with the coming of the Father Procurator of China, who is on the voyage. This letter of mine will reach Your Paternity on the feast of Saint Francis; I pray you to give him to me as companion, to follow him on so glorious a voyage. For the rest, I am praying Our Lord to grant every grace to Your Paternity, and I beseech you to give me your blessing, and the most desired placet to my most ardent desires."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most humble servant and son in the Lord — Leonardo Cinami. Massa, November 25, 1638."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Massa",
  "date_raw": "Massa, November 25, 1638",
  "date_only": "November 25, 1638",
  "year": 1638,
  "month": 11,
  "day": 25,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 142,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2846"
 },
 "99": {
  "number": 99,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Palermo, December 19, 1638",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 741, 289",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I had hoped by this hour to have begun, with the others, the so-longed-for Mission of the Indies, when I find myself unexpectedly shut out of so happy an election: and what most afflicts my soul is the doubt that such an opportunity will not offer itself again for the present, as this year's has to so many blessed Fathers and brothers; so that I confess to Your Paternity that, thinking on it, I cannot but weep for my failings, which doubtless have kept me from heaven's so-desired contentment; for in such wise has the desire been kindled day by day that no consolation can satisfy it save that which you alone with a nod can grant me; nor do I believe that the begun course of philosophy will restrain the charity of Your Paternity from fulfilling my desires to the full — which would indeed be to heap still more grief upon a soul so long anxious. With which I commend myself to you in the Holy Sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Mario Finechiaro. Palermo, December 19, 1638."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, December 19, 1638",
  "date_only": "December 19, 1638",
  "year": 1638,
  "month": 12,
  "day": 19,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 143,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2748"
 },
