 "101": {
  "number": 101,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Massa, July 20, 1639",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 99",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The most glorious death of Father Marcello Mastrilli has moved the whole world; and I believe that Your Paternity has received, on this occasion, thousands of letters from persons asking for the Indies. Mine come late, and after all the others, because they are ashamed to appear before you: first, for my sins, and second, for the many rejections. Nevertheless, though unworthy and rebuffed, I return to declare my desires once more — desires which will never end but with death. Now that the coming of the Father Procurator of China is awaited, asking it with these infinite [desires], I beg Your Paternity, for [the love] of so great glory of God and of the Society in those parts, not to rebuff me again. Let it suffice, as punishment for my shortcomings, that I once saw myself near to so great a grandeur and was cast down from it. Oh, if I could show Your Paternity the tears that for this cause I shed before God, how they would soften you! But I hope that Saint Francis Xavier will present them to you on my behalf, and will suggest to you those reasons that I do not know how to write to you. Wherewith, begging Your Paternity to give me your blessing, I end."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most obedient servant and son in the Lord — Leonardo Cinami. from Massa, the 20th of July 1639."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Massa",
  "date_raw": "Massa, July 20, 1639",
  "date_only": "July 20, 1639",
  "year": 1639,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 20,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 144,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2847"
 },
 "102": {
  "number": 102,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Palermo, October 16, 1639",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 168",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I do not know what more to take hold of in order to console in some part my soul, so greatly afflicted by the solicitude and anxiety of bringing to completion the ancient desire for the Mission of Japan; knowing especially that, although my desire grows and kindles more from hour to hour, the Lord does not deign to show me in Your Paternity's letters any ray of full and extraordinary hope. Often thinking upon this, I cannot but dissolve into most copious tears, attempting by this means to wash away altogether the filth of my sins, to which I attribute the long prolongation of the final execution of the said Mission. I console myself, on the other hand, by considering the fount whence I know so divine an inspiration to have derived to me; for I trust that the Most Holy Mother, who of her mere benignity deigned to transfuse into me so vehement a zeal for the conversion of that remote people, will also take it upon her to satisfy me altogether and to temper the flame of desire that continually consumes me — not ceasing also to beg Your Paternity to have compassion on a son of yours in Christ in a matter so pious, since with a single nod, if it please you, you could free me from so many anxieties and pains that bind me in such a manner. Nothing further occurring, I end only desiring that you consider this desire to be extraordinary, nor do I know how to console myself: wherewith I commend myself to your Holy Sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Mario Finechiaro. Palermo, the 16th of October 1639."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, October 16, 1639",
  "date_only": "October 16, 1639",
  "year": 1639,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 16,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 145,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2752"
 },
 "103": {
  "number": 103,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Massa, October 27, 1639",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 171",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Although for the present there seems no near opportunity for the Indies, nevertheless, to show my desire and the great affection that God deigns to communicate to me for this most glorious Mission, I return to make new instance of it to Your Paternity. I hope that so many and such importunate requests will not be burdensome to you, any more than prayers are to God; and I have faith that my letters, if they could have no efficacy on any other account, will at least have it by their multitude. Pardon, Your Paternity, for the love of God, my excessive importunity — which, though it be very great, is yet little in comparison with the infinite hunger I have to run into those provinces where God so long ago began to call me, and continues every day to call me. For the rest, let Your Paternity consider that only the hope of being consoled with the first Mission that shall go keeps me somewhat at peace in this life. Nor will the benignity of Your Paternity let me be left behind this next time, as my sins would deserve. Wherewith, begging Your Paternity to give me your blessing, I end."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most humble servant and son in the Lord — Leonardo Cinami. from Massa, the 27th of October 1639."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Massa",
  "date_raw": "Massa, October 27, 1639",
  "date_only": "October 27, 1639",
  "year": 1639,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 27,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 146,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2848"
 },
 "104": {
  "number": 104,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Massa, November 1, 1639",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 175",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I wish to renew with this letter all my ancient requests, and all the tears with which I have wearied for so long the most benign ears of Your Paternity. For the love of God, and by as much as you desire His glory, designate me for India; and do not answer me — my great desire makes me bold to set laws upon your charity — do not answer me that there is at present no opportunity, for Your Paternity can designate me now and advise me of when there shall be opportunity, which I hope will be soon. O God, why do I not find myself in Rome, cast at your most benign feet, showing my affection and the desire that has so long burned in my heart? Let Your Paternity at least consider it, and at last console me; and if you would show me a sign of your benignity, send me the crucifix that you were to send me when I was once before admonished — or at least declare that the crucifix I keep shall stand in place of the one owed to me for India. O how much I ask, and how little I deserve! Yet I do not repent of having asked. Meanwhile I beg my great Apostle and Captain Francis Xavier to plead my cause, since so many of my letters can avail so little by reason of my sins. Wherewith, humbly asking your blessing, I end."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most humble son and servant in the Lord — Leonardo Cinami. from Massa, on All Saints' Day 1639."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Massa",
  "date_raw": "Massa, November 1, 1639",
  "date_only": "November 1, 1639",
  "year": 1639,
  "month": 11,
  "day": 1,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 147,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2849"
 },
 "105": {
  "number": 105,
  "byline_raw": "Arganantio, Domenico — Noto, January 5, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 216",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I come with tears in my eyes to prostrate myself at the feet of Your Paternity, to kiss them a thousand and a thousand times with all reverence, as a respectful son desirous of being consoled by his dear father. I return to renew my wonted petition, for I cannot do otherwise, feeling myself continually urged to it by interior impulses. I know not what to do; my heart burns and consumes itself in living flames of desire to suffer for love of our Crucified One. I, Our Father, see very well how unworthy I am of a gift so matchless; but what shall I do, what shall I do? I feel impelled to ask for it, and those words of our Master in the Holy Gospel give me great confidence: petite, et accipietis, pulsate, et aperietur vobis (Ask, and you shall receive; knock, and it shall be opened to you.) Hence I shall never cease knocking until that most happy door is opened to me. Of the qualities required for these enterprises I find none in myself; I can only say that, by the grace of the Lord, I am in very good health; as to the spirit, I trust in the Lord that He will help me with His most holy grace; of the other qualities I know not what to say. I console myself in thinking that He who gave me the vocation will give me, of His infinite goodness, all that is required for it. The sole end of this request is the glory of the Lord, the salvation of my soul, the help of our neighbors, and to suffer what is sufferable for love of our Lord; so that — I am not ashamed to say it again and again — by God's grace I fear nothing for love of my Beloved: neither gallows, nor crosses, nor flames. Hasten, hasten, Our Father, for the love of the Lord and of the Blessed Virgin, console as soon as possible this son of yours, Your Paternity. I close the letter commending myself with all my heart to the holy blessing of Your Paternity and of the whole Society."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, your most unworthy son and servant — Domenico Arganantio. Noto, the 5th of January 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Arganantio, Domenico",
  "place_raw": "Noto",
  "date_raw": "Noto, January 5, 1640",
  "date_only": "January 5, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 148,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2720"
 },
 "106": {
  "number": 106,
  "byline_raw": "Amabilina, Marzio — Palermo, January 23, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 403",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I come with this my first letter, which the Lord inwardly dictates to me with much feeling and affection of my soul, to make known to Your Paternity, whom I hold as a loving Father, an ardent and heartfelt desire which, having effectually spurred me in the world to leave the world and embrace my Mother the Society, still goes on growing and strengthening in religion every day — such that to wish to try and examine it further, after so many trials and examinations, would seem to me to resist the Holy Spirit and not to answer God, who calls me so strongly; the more so as I feel a certain certainty and hope of obtaining what I long and desire for, since I long and desire it ardently — and of receiving from your paternal charity and most loving kindness that consolation than which, martyrdom excepted, I cannot in this life receive a greater; for Your Paternity wishes and delights to give every consolation and contentment to your sons who pray to you instantly and reverently. I therefore greatly desire to labor as much as I can for the salvation and conversion of souls, and to serve my Lord in Japan, or in some other part of the Indies, scattering my health, my blood, and my life for the love of Him who so prodigally scattered His own for me.",
   "The desire I have of this is not new, for the Lord communicated it to me of His goodness even when I was still in the world; nor is it ordinary, for having preserved and increased it as a religious, I have already reconfirmed and established it by a vow made with the license of my Spiritual Father, with whom I have conferred about everything. The reasons, moreover, that principally spur me to so great and noble a resolution are these. First, because the Lord gives me to understand that for the salvation of my soul there is no means safer, nor easier, nor more fitting than to go laboring through those spacious regions of the Indies, where so many souls miserably perish for lack of aid. Second, because I feel an unspeakable desire to suffer something for the love of my Lord, and it seems not a thing worthy of a soldier of Jesus to die idly in a bed, but, sweating and laboring for souls, to faint from sheer toil on a solitary shore, or to be imprisoned, burned, and bled to death for love of my God. Third, because this little time of life that remains to me I wish to spend on the thing most pleasing to God, and in an employment so glorious as the conversion of souls. Fourth, to follow the example of so many of my Fathers, who in this honorable calling shed their lives. These, and others, are the reasons that hold no small force in my soul — to which, if I made resistance, I would seem to resist the voices of God; the more so as I have no impediment that could hold me back, nor am I attached to anything sufficient to divert me; and leaving Sicily altogether, my country, my kinsmen, and my acquaintance, causes me no difficulty. Moreover I have already finished the course of philosophy, and this is my first year of school, which I am doing in Palermo; and I am already 22 years old, in good health and with strength well able to labor. Therefore, Our Father, I supplicate, I pray, and I adjure you — and oh, might it please the Lord that I could do so in person — by as much as you esteem the welfare and good of this your, albeit unworthy, son and servant, not to fail to console me and to fulfill this my most ardent and heartfelt desire. I await, therefore, and stand most ready at Your Paternity's nod, to whom, making most profound reverence and commending myself to your Holy Sacrifices, I end."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant in the Lord — Marzio Amabilina. Palermo, the 23rd of January 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Amabilina, Marzio",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, January 23, 1640",
  "date_only": "January 23, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 23,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 150,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2719"
 },
 "107": {
  "number": 107,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Palermo, January 29, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 233",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I wrote to Your Paternity last October recalling my ancient desire for the Mission of Japan; and no answer having come to hand, I write to you anew briefly, praying you earnestly to condescend to the longing of an unworthy son of yours in so just and pious a request. There is no need here to repeat, as I have always done since my Novitiate, the state of my said Vocation; it suffices that Your Paternity know only that every delay I accept as chastisement from the Lord in punishment of my sins. I therefore ask anew of Your Paternity, with all the affection of my heart, that you would finally consent to the sentiment, so kindled and strong, which from Heaven comes to me more firmly reconfirmed each day; for if you will, I do not believe a readier occasion could offer itself than the present one, which we are advised of from China by one of our Sicilian Fathers — namely, the coming of Father Alvaro Semedo, Procurator of that same China, to bring with him into those parts 100 subjects. I would go on further, but I end, seized by impatient grief, for it seemed to me that I could not, nor knew how to, explain my desire to Your Paternity — perhaps for this reason it has not been granted. I pray the Lord that He deign to be my interpreter before Your Paternity, to whom I shall remain with immortal gratitude if ever I am consoled by you. Wherewith I offer myself to you most resignedly, commending myself to your Holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant and son in Christ — Mario Finechiaro. from Palermo, the 29th of January 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, January 29, 1640",
  "date_only": "January 29, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 29,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 152,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2751"
 },
 "108": {
  "number": 108,
  "byline_raw": "Barbarito, Giovanni Antonio — Naples, February 4, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 237",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The offering of the Holy Child Jesus to His eternal Father in the Temple — whose feast we celebrated the day before yesterday — and that which, tomorrow being so many years since, our three holy Martyrs made to the same of their lives and blood, have moved me to renew in turn the offering that I have made at other times to the same Holy Child: of spending in His holy Service in the Indies the few days and few forces that remain to me in this miserable life, helping to cultivate that harvest, watered, whatever other cause there might be, with the Precious Blood of the blessed Christ; and to pray Your Paternity to deign to grant me this grace, which, as I have at other times hinted to Your Paternity, I esteem the greatest that I can receive in this life, after that which I hope for as the end of this one: holy Martyrdom. My Father General, do not, for the love of blessed God, regard my inabilities and imperfections, but only the dignity and worth of the personage who has been pleased to give me such a vocation — who, I hold evidently, is blessed God. And I promise him now, provided he grant me such a grace, to offer for Your Paternity a great part of what His Divine Majesty shall deign to let me suffer there. Wherewith, in the end making to Your Paternity most humble reverence, I pray you will give me your holy blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most humble servant, and most obedient son in the Lord — Giovanni Antonio Barbarito. Naples, the 4th of February 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Barbarito, Giovanni Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, February 4, 1640",
  "date_only": "February 4, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 4,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 153,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2721"
 },
 "109": {
  "number": 109,
  "byline_raw": "Arganantio, Domenico — Noto, February 18, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 245",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The time so longed for by me has already come, and so ardently asked of God Our Lord with so many prayers: now Your Paternity can satisfy these my kindled desires to suffer something for the love of my Lord. Our Father, let not Your Paternity abandon me in this fine opportunity of these happy tidings that come to us, by the Divine grace, from China. Oh, how my heart has been inflamed with living flames of desire to suffer for the love of my crucified Lord! Our Father, Our Father, with tears in my eyes I supplicate Your Paternity not to forget this your unworthy son. Most certain — and God knows how many my desires are — my heart boils as I write this. These are my desires; yet I am wholly conformed to the Will of the Lord, and indifferent to doing or omitting whatever Your Paternity shall judge in Domino (in the Lord) to be the greater glory of the Lord and the salvation of my soul."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant and son — Domenico Arganantio. Noto, the 18th of February 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Arganantio, Domenico",
  "place_raw": "Noto",
  "date_raw": "Noto, February 18, 1640",
  "date_only": "February 18, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 18,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 154,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2723"
 },
 "110": {
  "number": 110,
  "byline_raw": "Arisco, Lorenzo — Caltagirone, February 22, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 247",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Ever since my novitiate God Our Lord has given me a lively desire to ask for the Mission of the Indies; but because I was always told that such a thing was impossible to obtain for us brothers, I never proposed it to Your Paternity. Now, hearing that a great number of laborers is being sent to those parts, my longing has been the more kindled; wherefore, having communicated it to the Father Provincial, he tells me to take it up with Your Paternity and that he will not fail on his part to help me in what he can. Now I, Our Father, have recourse to Your Paternity, praying you with the greatest affection and efficacy possible to me, within the bounds of the indifference that holy Obedience requires of us, to console me also by placing me among the first who shall embark for those countries. The number of the Fathers, as I understand, is very great, whence it cannot be but that they still have need of some share of brothers. I am young in age and of very good strength; by profession a tailor, though I have done and offer to do each and every office wherein the superiors may wish to employ me. I have thought and thought again of how much adversity and travail could frighten me in such an enterprise, and against them all divine mercy gives me courage. So there remains nothing but to propose to Your Paternity, as I do with this present letter, this my Vocation, praying His Divine Majesty to deign to inspire you with what shall be in this matter His greater glory. Wherewith I commend myself greatly to the most holy sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant in Christ — Lorenzo Arisco. of Caltagirone, the 22nd of February 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Arisco, Lorenzo",
  "place_raw": "Caltagirone",
